title Season G fourteenth rung — X25519 key agreement in pure Rye
prompt Take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first past BUHR — Rye prioritized, Glow on green-witnessed Rye; read REMEMBER, the 1024-round itinerary, the six-season double-seat, and the rye-first crypto parity wave; send each finished increment to origin and xykj61; stop at custody gates.
think Compass walk: living edge is Season G (Cryptography), Ed25519 sign+verify whole at thirteen primitives. The decision-wave doc names Monocypher's set as Ed25519, X25519, BLAKE2b, and the ChaCha/Poly line; grep of crypto/ shows blake2b, sha512, chacha20, poly1305, aead, the full ed25519 family, and fe25519 all present — X25519 is the ONE named primitive not yet built. Lindy-first crux-first: X25519 serves every module that ever agrees a key (Comlink sessions, Vault sealed storage, two Ponds), the highest-Lindy remaining thread, and it is agent-doable (no custody gate — test scalars are not a key).
think The crux is already de-risked: X25519 is a Montgomery ladder that adds NO new field mathematics — it composes entirely over the GREEN base field crypto/fe25519.rye (add, sub, mul, square, invert all parity-proven), exactly as ed25519_scalarmul composed over the GREEN group law. The only new pieces are a constant-time cswap on limbs (XOR-mask, no branch on the secret), scalar clamping per RFC 7748, and a single multiply by a24 = 121665.
think Parity references chosen from what runs now: Zig 0.16 std.crypto.dh.X25519 exposes scalarmult(scalar, u) and recoverPublicKey(scalar) — an independent RFC 7748 implementation — and the std test file carries the published RFC 7748 section 5.2 known-answer vectors. Both anchor the selftest without any network fetch (Monocypher-source parity stays the held-fetch horizon the siblings carry).
think Authored crypto/x25519.rye: opening triad, version const, X25519Error set, field_one/field_zero/cswap/scalar_bit helpers each with two invariants, x25519(scalar,u) running the fixed 255-step RFC 7748 ladder (bit 254 down to 0) with the standard double-and-add schedule, x25519_base for the public-key case. Every fn carries >=2 // invariant asserts; the ladder's step count is bounded and value-independent.
obs First metal run failed only at KAT test 1 — I had mis-transcribed the vector1 output hex (dropped a byte, ...54b40755577a2855 for the true ...54b4075577a28552). Measurement caught memory: fixed the one byte from the std array, re-ran GREEN. The parity spread and Diffie-Hellman round-trip passed untouched, so the ladder itself was correct on the first pass; only the by-hand literal was wrong.
obs GREEN on crypto/x25519.rye: RFC 7748 section 5.2 vectors 1 and 2, the X25519(9,9) base vector (matching both x25519 and x25519_base), byte-for-byte parity vs std.crypto.dh.X25519 across 24 scalars and u-coordinates over arbitrary points AND the base point, and a two-party DH round-trip agreeing with the reference. tame_style_check bans clean; x25519.rye lands in none of the ratchet lists (no fn past 70 lines, asserts present).
obs Honest scope held: purely local over TEST scalars — no real private key, no real session, no network, no funds, no real device. Constant-time timing-safety is a named horizon (the ladder is data-independent by construction, yet timing is a measurement not a claim); agreeing a session with the maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate. With Ed25519 (sign+verify) and X25519 (key agreement) both GREEN, the full Monocypher-parity identity story now stands in pure Rye.
loom witness=crypto_x25519_selftest primitives=1 rfc7748_vectors=3 parity_pairs=24 dh_roundtrip=1 result=GREEN
file crypto/x25519.rye X25519 Diffie-Hellman authored in pure Rye over the GREEN base field, parity-GREEN vs RFC 7748 and std.crypto.dh.X25519
